  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table><thead><tr><th><div><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">Parameter</span></div></th><th><div><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">Result</span></div></th><th><div><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">Interpretation</span></div></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><div><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">Amylase</span></div></td><td><div><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">Normal</span></div></td><td><div><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">Amylase levels within the reference range indicate that the pancreas and salivary glands are functioning normally.</span></div></td></tr><tr><td><div><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">Amylase</span></div></td><td><div><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">High</span></div></td><td><div><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">Elevated amylase levels can be a sign of acute pancreatitis, pancreatic pseudocyst, pancreatic cancer, gallstones, or alcoholism. However, high amylase levels can also be caused by non-pancreatic conditions, such as kidney disease, mumps, or intestinal obstruction. Further diagnostic testing may be necessary to determine the underlying cause of high amylase levels.</span></div></td></tr><tr><td><div><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">Amylase</span></div></td><td><div><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">Low</span></div></td><td><div><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">Low amylase levels are rare and are not typically indicative of any specific medical condition. Low amylase levels may be seen in individuals with advanced chronic pancreatitis or pancreatic insufficiency.</span></div></td></tr></tbody></table><p><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">​​</span><br></p>",
